 \section SolarTheory Model Theory

 \subsection SolarPosTheory Position of the Sun

 The solar position model was implemented following the description in <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780123737502500069">Chapter 1 of Iqbal (1983)</a>.

 The day angle \f$\Gamma\f$ given as the polar angle of the earth relative to the sun (\f$\Gamma=0\f$ on Jan. 1) is calculated as

 <center>
 \f$\Gamma = 2\pi(DOY-1)/365\f$,  (1)
 </center>

 where DOY is the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_day">Julian Day</a> of the year.

 The solar declination angle is then calculated as

 <center>
 \f$\delta = 0.006918 - 0.399912\,\mathrm{cos}(\Gamma) + 0.070257\,\mathrm{sin}(\Gamma)- 0.006758\,\mathrm{cos}(2\Gamma) + 0.000907\,\mathrm{sin}(2\Gamma) - 0.002697\,\mathrm{cos}(3\Gamma) + 0.00148\,\mathrm{sin}(3\Gamma)\f$.         (2)
 </center>

 The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equation_of_time">equation of time</a> is calculated as
 
 <center>
 \f$EoT = 229.18(0.000075 + 0.001868\,\mathrm{cos}(\Gamma) - 0.032077\,\mathrm{sin}(\Gamma) - 0.014615\,\mathrm{cos}(2\Gamma) - 0.04089\,\mathrm{sin}(2\Gamma))\f$,         (3)
 </center>

 The hour angle is given by
 
 <center>
 \f$h=15(LST-12)\f$,         (4)
 </center>

 with

 <center>
   \f$LST=hour+minute/60+TC/60\f$,          (5)
 </center>

 and

 <center>
   \f$TC=4(15UTC-longitude)+EoT\f$,         (6)
 </center>

 Finally, the solar elevation angle is given by

 <center>
 \f$\theta_s=\mathrm{sin}^{-1}( \mathrm{sin}(latitude)\mathrm{sin}(\delta) + \mathrm{cos}(latitude)\mathrm{cos}(\delta)\mathrm{cos}(h) )\f$,         (7)
 </center>

 and the solar azimuthal angle is given by

 <center>
 \f$\phi_s=\mathrm{cos}^{-1}( (\mathrm{sin}(\delta) - \mathrm{sin}(\theta_s)\mathrm{sin}(latitude))/(\mathrm{cos}(\theta_s)\mathrm{cos}(latitude)))\f$.         (8)
 </center>

 Note that \f$\mathrm{cos}^{-1}\f$ gives angles between 0 and \f$\pi\f$, so to get a \f$\phi_s\f$ between 0 and \f$2\pi\f$, we take \f$\phi_s=2\pi-\phi_s\f$ if \f$LST>12\f$.

 \subsection SolarFluxTheory Direct and Diffuse Solar Flux (REST-2 Model)

 Clear-sky solar fluxes are calculated using the 'REST-2' (Reference Evaluation of Solar Transmittance, 2 bands) model of <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038092X07000990">Gueymard (2008)</a>. REST-2 is a high-performance broadband radiative transfer model derived from parameterizations of the SMARTS spectral code, and is widely recognized as one of the most accurate clear-sky models available.

 The model uses a two-band spectral scheme that separately treats the visible band (290-700 nm) and near-infrared band (700-4000 nm). In the visible band, attenuation is dominated by Rayleigh scattering and aerosol extinction, while the NIR band is primarily affected by water vapor absorption. For each band, the model calculates independent broadband transmittances for:

 - <b>Rayleigh scattering</b>: Molecular scattering by air molecules
 - <b>Uniformly mixed gases</b>: Absorption by CO<sub>2</sub> and O<sub>2</sub>
 - <b>Ozone</b>: UV and visible absorption bands
 - <b>Water vapor</b>: Major absorption in the NIR
 - <b>Aerosols</b>: Scattering and absorption characterized by Ångström turbidity coefficients

 Direct beam irradiance is computed from the product of these transmittances, while diffuse irradiance uses a two-layer scattering scheme that accounts for aerosol forward scattering and backscattering from the atmosphere-ground system. The model partitions the total radiative flux into direct and diffuse components suitable for agricultural, solar energy, and climate applications.

 \subsection SpectralIrradianceTheory Spectral Solar Irradiance (Model Based on SSolar-GOA)

 For applications requiring high spectral resolution (e.g., photosynthesis, remote sensing), \ref SolarPosition::calculateDirectSolarSpectrum(), \ref SolarPosition::calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum() and \ref SolarPosition::calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum() compute the direct normal, diffuse horizontal and global horizontal spectral irradiance at the ground from 300 to 2600 nm at 1 nm resolution. The model follows the structure of SSolar-GOA (<a href="https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-15-1689-2022">Cachorro et al. 2022</a>): the atmosphere is a single homogeneous layer of molecules and aerosol whose scattering transmittance is given by an analytical two-stream solution, gas absorption multiplies it, and multiple reflection between the ground and the atmosphere is included through the atmosphere's spherical albedo. It was implemented from the published equations, with the corrections and changes of data described below; equation numbers refer to Cachorro et al. (2022).

 <b>Extraterrestrial spectrum and geometry.</b> The extraterrestrial spectrum is the 1985 Wehrli standard spectrum (Wehrli, C., 1985, <em>Extraterrestrial Solar Spectrum</em>, Publication no. 615, Physikalisch-Meteorologisches Observatorium and World Radiation Center, Davos), as distributed by the <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/grid/solar-resource/spectra-wehrli">National Laboratory of the Rockies</a>. The unmodified file is included (<code>plugins/solarposition/assets/ssolar_goa/wehrli85.txt</code>, with the laboratory's data notice) and averaged over 1 nm bins centered on 300-2600 nm when it is loaded, each of its bins taken to extend halfway to its neighbours' centers. It is scaled for the Earth-Sun distance with the Fourier series of Spencer (1971, <em>Search</em> 2(5):172). The relative optical air mass is that of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.28.004735">Kasten and Young (1989)</a>,

 \f[
 m = \frac{1}{\sin h + 0.50572\,(h + 6.07995)^{-1.6364}},
 \f]

 with the solar elevation \f$h\f$ in degrees, in place of the plane-parallel air mass \f$m = 1/\cos\theta_s\f$ used in the equations of Cachorro et al. (p. 1693), which diverges as the sun approaches the horizon; Cachorro et al. note that more accurate expressions, among them that of Kasten and Young, are needed above 70 degrees. The same air mass is used for scattering and for gas absorption.

 <b>Optical depths.</b> The Rayleigh optical depth is Eq. 7, which Cachorro et al. attribute to Gueymard (1995), scaled by the ratio of the surface pressure to 101325 Pa:

 \f[
 \tau_R = \frac{P/P_0}{117.2594\lambda^4 - 1.3215\lambda^2 + 0.00032 - 0.000076\lambda^{-2}},
 \f]

 with \f$\lambda\f$ in \f$\mu\f$m. The aerosol optical depth follows the Ångström law (Eq. 8), \f$\tau_a = \beta\lambda^{-\alpha}\f$, with the turbidity set by \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions() as \f$\beta\f$ and \f$\alpha = 1.3\f$. The aerosol single-scattering albedo \f$\omega_a = 0.893\f$ and asymmetry parameter \f$g_a = 0.634\f$ are those of the 6S continental aerosol at 550 nm (6S User Guide, Version 3, Part 2, Table 3, p. 120) and are applied at all wavelengths. Versions before 1.3.90 used 0.90 and 0.85, values of undocumented origin that are not given in Cachorro et al. (2022).

 <b>Scattering transmittance.</b> With \f$\tau_t = \tau_R + \tau_a\f$, the layer's single-scattering albedo is Eq. 16, \f$\omega = (\tau_R + \omega_a\tau_a)/\tau_t\f$. Its asymmetry parameter is \f$g = \omega_a\tau_a g_a/(\tau_R + \omega_a\tau_a)\f$ (Rayleigh scattering has \f$g = 0\f$): the phase function of a mixture is the average of its components' phase functions weighted by their scattering coefficients, and \f$g\f$ is its first moment (defined in the 6S User Guide, Part 2, p. 117). The printed Eq. 16, \f$g_a\tau_a/\tau_t\f$, weights by extinction instead and omits \f$\omega_a\f$. The total (direct plus diffuse) transmittance of the layer over a black ground is Eq. 14,

 \f[
 T = \frac{(1 - r_0^2)\,e^{-k\tau_t m}}{1 - r_0^2\,e^{-2k\tau_t m}}, \qquad r_0 = \frac{k - 1 + \omega}{k + 1 - \omega}, \qquad k = \sqrt{(1 - \omega)(1 - \omega g)}.
 \f]

 The printed Eq. 15 has no square root in \f$k\f$, which makes \f$r_0\f$ negative for any forward-scattering layer. With the square root, \f$r_0 = (1 - s)/(1 + s)\f$ with \f$s = \sqrt{(1-\omega)/(1-\omega g)}\f$, the reflectance of a semi-infinite layer in the two-stream approximation with the hemispheric closure (the ratio \f$\zeta_-/\zeta_+\f$ of Eq. 72 of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/0067-0049/215/1/4">Heng et al. 2014</a>). The direct transmittance is \f$e^{-\tau_t m}\f$ (Eqs. 3-5).

 <b>Spherical albedo.</b> Multiple reflection between the ground (albedo \f$\rho\f$, set with \ref SolarPosition::setGroundAlbedo()) and the atmosphere multiplies the global irradiance by \f$1/(1 - \rho S)\f$ (Eq. 17, after Lenoble 1998), where \f$S\f$ is the atmosphere's spherical albedo; it does not affect the direct beam. For a purely Rayleigh atmosphere, \f$S\f$ is the closed form of 6S subroutine CSALBR (6S User Guide, Part 2, p. 96),

 \f[
 S_R = \frac{3\tau_R - 4E_3(\tau_R) + 6E_4(\tau_R)}{4 + 3\tau_R},
 \f]

 where \f$E_n\f$ are exponential integrals. It equals \f$1 - 2\int_0^1 \mu\,T(\mu)\,d\mu\f$ for the Rayleigh transmittance \f$T(\mu) = [(2/3 + \mu) + (2/3 - \mu)e^{-\tau_R/\mu}]/(4/3 + \tau_R)\f$ of 6S subroutine SCATRA (p. 135). The printed Eq. 19, \f$\tau_R(1 - e^{-2\tau_R})/(2 + \tau_R)\f$, grows as \f$\tau_R^2\f$ for a thin layer instead of as \f$\tau_R\f$ and is about a tenth of this value at 550 nm. Eq. 18 adds a separate aerosol albedo to \f$S_R\f$, which, compared with 6S, overstates the albedo of the mixture at short wavelengths and high aerosol load because the aerosol shields part of the Rayleigh layer. The model instead adds the change in the two-stream reflectance of the whole layer caused by the aerosol:

 \f[
 S = S_R + R(\tau_t, \omega, g) - R(\tau_R, 1, 0), \qquad R = \frac{\zeta_-\zeta_+(1 - \mathcal{T}^2)}{\zeta_+^2 - \zeta_-^2\mathcal{T}^2}, \qquad \zeta_\pm = \frac{1 \pm s}{2}, \qquad \mathcal{T} = 2E_3\!\left(\tau\sqrt{(1-\omega)(1-\omega g)}\right),
 \f]

 where \f$R\f$ is the reflectance of a layer under diffuse illumination in the two-stream approximation (Eq. 1 of <a href="https://doi.org/10.3847/1538-4365/aa8907">Heng and Kitzmann 2017</a>, with the transmission function for diffuse illumination of their Sect. 2), which tends to \f$(1-g)\tau/(1 + (1-g)\tau)\f$ for \f$\omega = 1\f$. This combination is not a published formula. It reproduces \f$S_R\f$ exactly without aerosol, and it was chosen over Eq. 18 by comparison with 6S (see the validation below).

 <b>Gas absorption.</b> Absorption by water vapor, ozone and the uniformly mixed gases (oxygen, carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide and carbon monoxide) is interpolated from gas transmittance tables computed with 6S at 1 nm spacing (<code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/generate_atmosphere_lut.py</code>), as functions of the water vapor path (the precipitable water times \f$m\f$), the ozone path (the ozone column times \f$m\f$) and \f$m\f$ times the ratio of the surface pressure to 1013.25 hPa, respectively. As in SSolar-GOA, the gas transmittance along the solar path multiplies both the direct and the diffuse irradiance. The tables extend to a pressure-weighted air mass of 37.99 (the air mass at the horizon is 37.92), a water vapor path of 320 g/cm² and an ozone path of 25 atm-cm. A longer path raises an error; with the sun close to the horizon this happens for precipitable water above about 8.4 cm, an ozone column above about 660 DU, or a surface pressure above about 1015 hPa. Below the smallest path of each table (for the uniformly mixed gases a pressure-weighted air mass of 0.4986, reached only for surface pressures below about 505 hPa), the transmittance is interpolated linearly toward one at zero path. SSolar-GOA instead uses band-model parameterizations for water vapor (Pierluissi et al. 1989, with coefficients from LOWTRAN7) and oxygen (Pierluissi and Maragoudakis 1986) and measured cross sections for ozone, and neglects the other gases.

 The irradiances are then

 \f[
 E_{dir} = E_0\,T_{gas}\,e^{-\tau_t m}, \qquad E_{glb} = \frac{\cos\theta_s\,E_0\,T_{gas}\,T}{1 - \rho S}, \qquad E_{dif} = E_{glb} - \cos\theta_s\,E_{dir},
 \f]

 where \f$E_0\f$ is the extraterrestrial irradiance corrected for the Earth-Sun distance, \f$T_{gas}\f$ the product of the gas transmittances and \f$\theta_s\f$ the solar zenith angle. Cloud calibration, if enabled, is then applied (see \ref SolarFluxCloudsSpectral).

 <b>Parameter derivation.</b> The inputs are those of the REST-2 model (pressure, temperature, humidity, turbidity). The precipitable water is derived from the temperature and humidity through the dew point (a relation attributed in an earlier implementation to Viswanadham 1981), and the ozone column comes from a satellite climatology unless set by the user (see \ref OzoneColumn). The ground albedo is 0.2 unless set with \ref SolarPosition::setGroundAlbedo().

 <b>Validation against 6S.</b> The model was compared with 6S (6SV1.1, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/36.581987">Vermote et al. 1997</a>) for the continental aerosol at sea level, with the same water vapor (1.18 cm) and ozone (318 DU) columns, at 14 wavelengths from 350 to 2200 nm, solar zenith angles of 0, 30, 60 and 75 degrees, aerosol optical depths at 550 nm from 0 to 1 and ground albedos of 0, 0.2 and 0.8 (<code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/compare_spectral_with_6s.py</code>). Irradiances were compared as transmittances, so the difference between the two codes' extraterrestrial spectra does not enter. The table gives the range of the relative differences from 6S for 400-2200 nm (excluding 1040 nm) and solar zenith angles up to 60 degrees; the 350 nm, 1040 nm and 75 degree cases are described below it.

 <table>
 <tr><th>Aerosol optical depth at 550 nm</th><th>0</th><th>0.05</th><th>0.2</th><th>0.5</th><th>1</th></tr>
 <tr><td>Direct normal</td><td>-0.05 to +0.7%</td><td>-0.9 to +0.6%</td><td>-5.5 to +2.6%</td><td>-14 to +7%</td><td>-27 to +14%</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Global horizontal, ground albedo 0.2</td><td>-0.05 to +0.3%</td><td>-0.3 to +0.5%</td><td>-1.2 to +2.0%</td><td>-2.2 to +5.3%</td><td>-5.5 to +11%</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Global horizontal, ground albedo 0.8</td><td>-0.2 to +0.1%</td><td>-1.2 to +0.5%</td><td>-1.6 to +2.0%</td><td>-1.7 to +5.3%</td><td>-4.1 to +12%</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Diffuse horizontal, ground albedo 0.2</td><td>-1.1 to +2.0%</td><td>-16 to +1.7%</td><td>-17 to +4.3%</td><td>-15 to +6.6%</td><td>-13 to +12%</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Spherical albedo (absolute), all wavelengths</td><td>-0.007 to 0</td><td>-0.019 to +0.001</td><td>-0.013 to +0.002</td><td>-0.001 to +0.009</td><td>+0.007 to +0.019</td></tr>
 </table>

 Without aerosol the model agrees with 6S to within 0.7% for the direct and global irradiance. With aerosol, most of the difference comes from the aerosol model rather than the equations: the continental aerosol's optical properties vary with wavelength (from 0.4 to 0.86 \f$\mu\f$m its extinction corresponds to an Ångström exponent of about 1.16, and its single-scattering albedo falls to 0.75 at 1.54 \f$\mu\f$m; 6S User Guide, Part 2, Table 3), whereas the model uses \f$\alpha = 1.3\f$ and the 550 nm values of \f$\omega_a\f$ and \f$g_a\f$ at all wavelengths. This is why the direct normal irradiance differs by up to 27% at an aerosol optical depth of 1, and why, for a ground albedo of 0.2, the diffuse irradiance in the near infrared (1240-2200 nm) is 9-17% lower than 6S at aerosol optical depths of 0.05-0.2 and solar zenith angles of 0 and 30 degrees (0.3-12% lower at 60 degrees); from 400 to 870 nm, at solar zenith angles up to 60 degrees, the diffuse irradiance agrees to within 8.6% at those aerosol loads. With 6S's own aerosol optical depth, single-scattering albedo and asymmetry parameter at each wavelength, the spherical albedo of the model differs from that of 6S by at most 0.017 (350-2130 nm, aerosol optical depths 0.05-1), which changes the global irradiance by at most 1.9% for a ground albedo of 0.8 and 0.4% for a ground albedo of 0.2. For comparison, the additive form of Eq. 18 (with the same two-stream aerosol reflectance) overstates the spherical albedo by up to 0.23 in the same comparison, which raises the global irradiance by up to 34% at 350 nm for a ground albedo of 0.8 and an aerosol optical depth of 1, by 7.7% at 450 nm and an aerosol optical depth of 0.5, and by up to 5.2% for a ground albedo of 0.2. The error grows with the ground albedo, since it enters through \f$1/(1 - \rho S)\f$.

 The differences are larger at a solar zenith angle of 75 degrees, where the two-stream solution is least accurate: from 400 to 2200 nm (excluding 1040 nm) the diffuse irradiance differs from 6S by -4.9% to +14% at aerosol optical depths up to 0.2, and the global irradiance by -5.4% to +6.9% (ground albedo 0.2); at an aerosol optical depth of 0.5 the global irradiance differs by -17% to +15% and the diffuse irradiance by -17% to +18% at the wavelengths tested by the self-test (400, 450, 550, 670, 870, 1240 and 1640 nm). At 350 nm without aerosol, the direct irradiance is higher than that of 6S by up to 5.1% at a solar zenith angle of 75 degrees (partly because the Kasten-Young air mass there, 3.81, is smaller than the plane-parallel air mass of 6S, 3.86), whereas the global irradiance is lower by up to 4.1% (ground albedo 0.2); at an aerosol optical depth of 0.2 the global and diffuse irradiance at 350 nm are lower by up to 15%. At 1040 nm without aerosol, the model's diffuse irradiance is 25-31% higher than that of 6S (ground albedo 0.2). 6S computes its atmospheric functions at 20 wavelengths and interpolates them in wavelength as power laws (6S User Guide, Part 2, pp. 97 and 105), and without aerosol and gas absorption its diffuse transmittance divided by the Rayleigh optical depth (overhead sun, black ground) is 0.50 at 860 and 1240 nm but 0.36 at 1040 nm, a dip of about 28% where Rayleigh scattering should vary smoothly; the difference therefore reflects 6S's interpolation rather than the model. A self-test compares the model with 6S values generated by <code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/generate_ssolar_reference.py</code>.

 <b>Output Format:</b> Results are stored in Context global data as vectors of (wavelength, irradiance) pairs (\ref helios::vec2) with user-defined labels. Three spectral components are computed: global irradiance on horizontal surface, direct irradiance normal to sun direction, and diffuse irradiance on horizontal surface (all in W/m²/nm).

 \subsection SensorAtmosphereTheory Atmosphere Between the Scene and a Satellite Sensor

 To simulate imagery from a sensor above the atmosphere (e.g., a satellite), the radiance leaving the scene must be converted to the radiance reaching the sensor. The atmosphere attenuates the scene radiance and adds light of its own. Following the formulation of the 6S radiative transfer code (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/36.581987">Vermote et al. 1997</a>), the radiance at the sensor is

 \f$L_{sensor} = L_{path} + T_{dir}^{\uparrow} L_{surface} + T_{dif}^{\uparrow} \rho_g E_g / \pi\f$,

 where \f$L_{path}\f$ is the path radiance scattered into the sensor by the atmosphere without reaching the ground, \f$T_{dir}^{\uparrow}\f$ and \f$T_{dif}^{\uparrow}\f$ are the direct and diffuse transmittances from the ground to the sensor, and the last term (the adjacency effect) is light reflected by the surrounding ground of albedo \f$\rho_g\f$, lit by the global irradiance \f$E_g\f$, and scattered into the sensor's line of sight. The global irradiance includes multiple reflection between the ground and the atmosphere through the atmosphere's spherical albedo \f$S\f$: \f$E_g = \mu_s E_0 T_g^{\downarrow} T^{\downarrow} / (1 - \rho_g S)\f$.

 Path radiance comes from multiple scattering by molecules and aerosol, which single-scattering approximations do not capture. The scattering quantities (path reflectance, total transmittance, optical depth and spherical albedo) are therefore taken from a look-up table computed with the vector version of 6S (release 6SV1.1; see <a href="https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.45.006762">Kotchenova et al. 2006</a> for its validation) for its continental aerosol model. They were computed without gas absorption at 16 wavelengths from 300 to 2600 nm and are interpolated log-log in wavelength between them. The table spans surface pressures from 701.2 to 1013 hPa, aerosol optical depths at 550 nm from 0 to 1.2, solar zenith angles up to 70 degrees, view zenith angles up to 60 degrees and all relative azimuths. Transmittance by water vapor, ozone and the uniformly mixed gases (oxygen, carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide and carbon monoxide), also from 6S, is tabulated separately at 1 nm spacing as a function of the water vapor path, the ozone path and the air mass times the ratio of the surface pressure to 1013.25 hPa, respectively. Radiance scattered by the atmosphere is attenuated by the absorption of half of the water vapor path, an approximation for water vapor concentrated near the ground that agrees with 6S in the comparison below.

 The top-of-atmosphere reflectance of a uniform Lambertian surface was compared with direct 6S calculations for 9984 cases: surface pressures of 1013 and 845 hPa (sea level and 1.5 km), solar zenith angles of 15, 35, 55 and 68 degrees, view zenith angles of 0, 25, 45 and 58 degrees, relative azimuths of 0, 45, 110 and 165 degrees (a single azimuth at nadir), aerosol optical depths at 550 nm of 0.03, 0.15, 0.4 and 0.9, surface reflectances of 0.05 and 0.3, 12 wavelengths from 470 to 2250 nm, and one water vapor column (1.18 cm), ozone column and date. The mean absolute difference was 0.0009 and the maximum 0.011, at the largest aerosol optical depth (0.9) with the sun at 68 degrees and the sensor at 58 degrees. Excluding the absorption bands at 940, 1375 and 1900 nm, the maximum difference was 0.0054 for aerosol optical depths up to 0.4. The comparison can be repeated with <code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/compare_with_6s.py</code>.

 Limitations: a single (continental) aerosol type; the sensor is above the atmosphere; the adjacency effect assumes surroundings of uniform albedo; and clear sky.

 \subsection SensorThermalAtmosphereTheory Thermal Infrared Atmosphere Between the Scene and a Satellite Sensor

 In a thermal infrared band the atmosphere absorbs part of the radiance leaving the scene and emits radiance of its own, so the spectral radiance at a sensor above the atmosphere is \f$L_{sensor} = \tau L_{surface} + L^{\uparrow}\f$, where \f$\tau\f$ is the atmospheric transmittance along the view path and \f$L^{\uparrow}\f$ the radiance emitted by the atmosphere along it. The atmosphere also emits the sky radiance that the scene reflects; it is described by \f$L^{\downarrow}\f$, the downwelling irradiance at the ground divided by \f$\pi\f$, so that a Lambertian surface of emissivity \f$\varepsilon\f$ reflects \f$(1-\varepsilon)L^{\downarrow}\f$.

 <b>Look-up table.</b> The three quantities are interpolated from a look-up table (<code>plugins/solarposition/assets/thermal_atmosphere_lut/</code>) computed with the radiative transfer tool uvspec of libRadtran 2.0.6 (<a href="https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-9-1647-2016">Emde et al. 2016</a>) using the REPTRAN molecular absorption parameterization at its finest resolution, 1 cm⁻¹ (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2014.06.024">Gasteiger et al. 2014</a>), and the DISORT solver, for a clear sky without aerosol and a plane-parallel atmosphere. REPTRAN includes absorption by water vapor, carbon dioxide, ozone, nitrous oxide, carbon monoxide, methane, oxygen, nitrogen and nitrogen dioxide, from HITRAN 2004 line data with the MT_CKD 1.0 continuum. The 1 cm⁻¹ results are averaged over 5 cm⁻¹ bins from 650 to 1820 cm⁻¹ (5.49-15.38 \f$\mu\f$m) and stored at the bin centers, 5502-15326 nm, with radiances per nm. \f$\tau\f$ and \f$L^{\uparrow}\f$ are separated by computing each atmosphere over a black surface at two temperatures: without scattering the radiance at the top of the atmosphere is \f$\tau B(T_s) + L^{\uparrow}\f$, so the difference of the two radiances gives \f$\tau\f$. The table was generated with <code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/generate_thermal_atmosphere_lut.py</code>.

 <b>Atmospheric profiles.</b> The temperature and humidity profiles are not known from the surface conditions alone, so the table assumes climatological ones: the tropical, midlatitude summer, U.S. Standard, midlatitude winter and subarctic winter atmospheres of <a href="https://www.osti.gov/biblio/6862535">Anderson et al. (1986, AFGL-TR-86-0110)</a>, whose surface air temperatures are 299.7, 294.2, 288.2, 272.2 and 257.2 K. For a given surface air temperature the temperature, water vapor and ozone profiles are interpolated linearly between the two atmospheres whose temperatures at the surface bracket it; below the coldest and above the warmest atmosphere, that atmosphere is shifted by the temperature difference (in full up to 10 km, tapering to zero at 15 km). The profile is cut at the surface pressure, its water vapor and ozone are scaled to the given columns, and carbon dioxide (425.62 ppm), methane (1935.94 ppb) and nitrous oxide (338.85 ppb) are set to the 2025 global annual means of the <a href="https://gml.noaa.gov/ccgg/trends/">NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory</a>, in place of the 1986 amounts of Anderson et al. (carbon dioxide 330 ppm). This is a simplification made for this model. Operational products instead use measured or reanalysis profiles; for example, the Landsat Collection 2 surface temperature product uses reanalysis profiles at 42 pressure levels (<a href="https://d9-wret.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/assets/palladium/production/s3fs-public/media/files/LSDS-1747_Landsat8-9_Calibration-Validation_Algorithm-Description-Document_v5.pdf">Landsat 8-9 Calibration and Validation Algorithm Description Document, LSDS-1747</a>).

 The table's axes are the column water vapor (0.1-7.5 g/cm², interpolated in its logarithm), the position of the surface air temperature in the climatology (at sea level, 246.2-321.7 K; the range is lower at higher elevations, e.g. 242.4-304.6 K at 701.2 hPa), the surface pressure (701.2-1013 hPa, about 0-3 km, extrapolated linearly up to 1050 hPa), and the secant of the view zenith angle (0-60 degrees). The ozone column (150-450 DU) is applied as a correction: most ozone lies above the water vapor, so the radiance at the top of the atmosphere for an ozone column other than 300 DU is \f$R(\tau B + L^{\uparrow}) + D\f$, with the transmittance ratio \f$R\f$ and the emission term \f$D\f$ tabulated against the climate, surface pressure, ozone column and view angle (computed at 1 g/cm² of water vapor; in a test at sea level, a correction computed at 1.44 g/cm² reproduced the effect of changing the ozone column from 300 to 200 or 450 DU at 0.2 and 5 g/cm² to within 0.05 K in brightness temperature). \f$L^{\downarrow}\f$ is tabulated with the ozone column as an axis. Ozone affects not only its 9.6 \f$\mu\f$m band but also 650-815 cm⁻¹, 1100-1200 cm⁻¹ and parts of 1695-1820 cm⁻¹. The table is 6.0 MB.

 <b>Accuracy.</b> The comparisons below are for brightness temperatures at the top of the atmosphere integrated over the bands in the table at the end of this section (boxcar in wavelength), over a black surface at the air temperature.
 <ul>
 <li><i>Radiative transfer.</i> For seven atmospheres (the five climatological ones at their own water vapor columns, a dry atmosphere over a 2 km surface, and a humid one with 6 g/cm² at 305 K), at nadir and at 60 degrees, uvspec with REPTRAN agrees with the line-by-line model LBLRTM 12.17 (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2004.05.058">Clough et al. 2005</a>; AER line file 3.8.1, MT_CKD 4.3 continuum) given the same profiles. The mean absolute difference is 0.16-0.24 K in the window bands between 8 and 13.5 \f$\mu\f$m (largest 0.75 K, in the most humid atmospheres, where REPTRAN transmits less than LBLRTM), 0.29-0.34 K in the water vapor bands, 0.37-0.44 K in the carbon dioxide bands (MODIS 33 and 36, ABI 16; largest 0.78 K) and 0.40-0.43 K in the ozone bands (MODIS 30, ABI 12; largest 0.96 K). The differences in the water vapor and ozone bands are larger at 60 degrees than at nadir, where LBLRTM's spherical geometry and uvspec's plane-parallel geometry differ most. The comparison can be repeated with <code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/compare_thermal_with_lblrtm.py</code>.</li>
 <li><i>Interpolation.</i> For 94 random states between the table's nodes (air temperature 250-305 K, water vapor 0.1-7.5 g/cm², surface pressure 701-1013 hPa, ozone 150-450 DU, view zenith 0-60 degrees), the table as used by \ref SolarPosition::calculateSensorThermalAtmosphere() differs from direct uvspec calculations by 0.03-0.09 K root-mean-square in the window bands between 8 and 13.5 \f$\mu\f$m (largest 0.47 K), at most 0.10 K root-mean-square in the carbon dioxide bands, 0.13-0.25 K in the water vapor bands (largest 0.51 K) and 0.12-0.17 K in the ozone bands (largest 0.80 K). \f$L^{\downarrow}\f$ integrated over a band differs by at most 2.6% root-mean-square (largest 6.2%, in the window bands, where the sky radiance is smallest). The comparison can be repeated with <code>plugins/solarposition/utilities/compare_thermal_with_uvspec.py</code>.</li>
 <li><i>Profile assumption.</i> The largest source of error for a real scene is usually the difference between its atmosphere and the assumed profile. For the subarctic summer atmosphere of Anderson et al., which is not one of the table's base atmospheres (287.2 K, 2.12 g/cm² of water vapor), the table's profile at the same surface air temperature and water vapor and ozone columns gives brightness temperatures 0.2-0.5 K lower in the window bands between 8 and 12.5 \f$\mu\f$m (0.7 K for a 7.5-13.5 \f$\mu\f$m band), 1.4-2.4 K lower in the ozone bands and in MODIS 33 and ABI 16, and 3.8-6.9 K lower in the bands that sense the upper troposphere and stratosphere (ABI 8 and 10, MODIS 27 and 36) (<code>compare_thermal_with_uvspec.py --held-out</code>). Sounding bands therefore reflect the assumed climatological profile rather than the actual one.</li>
 <li><i>Earlier model.</i> Before the table, this model used the generalized atmospheric functions of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2003JD003480">Jiménez-Muñoz and Sobrino (2003)</a> (corrected in <a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2004JD004804">2004</a>), fitted to MODTRAN 3.5 simulations of about 60 radiosoundings for effective wavelengths of 10-12 \f$\mu\f$m, which depend on the water vapor column only. Applying those functions to invert the radiance given by the table (nadir, surface at the air temperature, relative humidity 0.2-0.9) retrieves the surface temperature to within 1.3 K for ASTER band 14, MODIS bands 31 and 32, Landsat 8/9 TIRS bands 10 and 11 and Landsat TM/ETM+ band 6 when the water vapor is at most 1.5 g/cm² and the air temperature at most 290 K, which is within the accuracy the authors report for their functions (root-mean-square errors below 1.5 K for TM band 6 and below 2 K for AVHRR channel 4 and ATSR-2 channel 2). For ASTER band 13 the difference is 2.1-3.3 K under the same conditions. For warmer and more humid air the differences grow, to several kelvin and beyond 20 K for MODIS 32 and TIRS 11 at 5-6 g/cm² and 310 K, because the functions cannot represent the air temperature (<code>compare_thermal_with_jms.py</code>).</li>
 </ul>

 Limitations: clear sky without aerosol; the sensor is above the atmosphere; the atmosphere is plane-parallel; the profiles are climatological (see above); and bands at 3.5-5 \f$\mu\f$m, where reflected sunlight is significant, are not covered.

 <b>Satellite thermal bands.</b> Any band within 5502-15326 nm can be used. The table below lists thermal bands of NASA, USGS and NOAA sensors and their limits as published by the instrument teams, which define them in different ways; where a spectral response function is available it can be set as the camera's spectral response (see RadiationModel::setCameraSpectralResponse()).
