 \subsection LWTheory Ambient Longwave Flux

 The broadband longwave radiation flux emitted by a clear sky (\ref SolarPosition::getAmbientLongwaveFlux()) is modeled following <a href="https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/qj.49712253306">Prata (1996)</a>, which gives the effective emissivity of the sky as a function of the precipitable water in the atmosphere:

 <center>
   \f$\epsilon_s = 1-(1+u)\mathrm{exp}\left(-\left(1.2+3u\right)^{0.5}\right)\f$,
 </center>

 where \f$u\f$ is the precipitable water in cm. This implementation estimates it from the air temperature \f$T_a\f$ (K) and relative humidity \f$h\f$ set with \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions() as \f$u = 0.465\,e_0/T_a\f$, with the vapor pressure \f$e_0 = 611\,h\,\mathrm{exp}\left(17.502\,(T_a-273)/(T_a-273+240.9)\right)\f$ in Pa. (The precipitable water used by the spectral solar models and the thermal atmosphere look-up table is instead derived from the dew point; see \ref SolarPosition::calculateSensorThermalAtmosphere().)

 The downwelling longwave radiation flux on a horizontal surface is given by

 <center>
   \f$R_L=\epsilon_s\sigma T_a^4\f$,
 </center>

 where \f$\sigma=5.67\times10^{-8}\f$ W/m<sup>2</sup>-K<sup>4</sup>, and \f$T_a\f$ is the air temperature in Kelvin measured near the ground (say 2 m height).

 This flux covers all longwave wavelengths. It is not the right diffuse flux for an emission band with wavelength bounds, which should receive only the sky radiation within the band: \ref SolarPosition::getThermalSkyFlux() computes that from the thermal atmosphere look-up table (see \ref SensorThermalAtmosphereTheory), for bands within 5502-15326 nm. The table cannot replace the broadband flux, since much of the sky's longwave emission lies outside that range (e.g., the water vapor rotation band beyond 15 \f$\mu\f$m).

 \section SolarLib Using the SolarPosition Plug-in

 \subsection SolarPos Getting the Direction of the Sun

 The direction of the sun can be queried in one of several ways: a Cartesian unit vector pointing in the direction of the sun, a spherical coordinate describing the direction of the sun, the elevation angle of the sun, the zenithal angle of the sun, and the azimuthal angle of the sun.  The functions to query these quantities are given in the table below. Each of these functions calculates the solar direction based on the current time and date set in the Context (see \ref helios::Context::setTime() "setTime()" and \ref helios::Context::setDate() "setDate()"), and the UTC, latitude, and longitude specified in the \ref SolarPosition constructor.

 <table>
 <tr><th>Direction Quantity</th><th>Function</th></tr>
 <tr><td>Unit vector pointing toward the sun.</td><td>\ref SolarPosition::getSunDirectionVector()</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Spherical coordinate vector pointing toward the sun.</td><td>\ref SolarPosition::getSunDirectionSpherical()</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Elevation angle of the sun (radians).</td><td>\ref SolarPosition::getSunElevation()</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Zenithal angle of the sun (radians).</td><td>\ref SolarPosition::getSunZenith()</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Azimuthal angle of the sun (radians).</td><td>\ref SolarPosition::getSunAzimuth()</td></tr>
 </table>

 Below is an example of how to use the \ref SolarPosition mode to calculate the sun angle.

 ~~~~~~
  #include "SolarPosition.h"

  int main(){
      Context context; //declare the context

      //Set the current time and date
      context.setDate(1,5,2015); //May 1, 2015
      context.setTime(30,12); //12:30

      //Initialize the SolarPosition class
      SolarPosition sun( 7, 31.256, 119.947, &context );

      //Get the sun position
      vec3 direction = sun.getSunDirectionVector(); //unit vector

      float elevation = sun.getSunElevation(); //elevation angle (radians)
      float azimuth = sun.getSunAzimuth(); //azimuthal angle (radians)
      
  }
 ~~~~~~

 \subsection AtmosphericConditions Setting Atmospheric Conditions Globally

 The SolarPosition plugin supports setting atmospheric conditions globally in the Context, which allows them to be easily shared across different plugins. This is the recommended approach for setting atmospheric parameters.

 To use this approach, first set the atmospheric conditions using the \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions() method. This stores the atmospheric parameters as global data in the Context with the labels: atmosphere_pressure_Pa, atmosphere_temperature_K, atmosphere_humidity_rel, and atmosphere_turbidity.

 ~~~~~~
  #include "SolarPosition.h"

  int main(){
      Context context; //declare the context

      //Set the current time and date
      context.setDate(1,5,2015); //May 1, 2015
      context.setTime(30,12); //12:30

      //Initialize the SolarPosition class
      SolarPosition sun( 7, 31.256, 119.947, &context );

      //Set atmospheric conditions globally
      sun.setAtmosphericConditions( 101000, 300, 0.6, 0.05 ); //pressure, temperature, humidity, turbidity

      //Calculate solar flux using stored atmospheric conditions
      float R = sun.getSolarFlux(); //flux perpendicular to sun (W/m^2)
      float zenith = sun.getSunZenith(); //zenithal angle (radians)
      float R_horiz = R*cos(zenith);  //flux on horizontal surface

      float f_diff = sun.getDiffuseFraction(); //fraction of diffuse radiation

      float R_dir = R*(1.f-f_diff); //direct component of flux (W/m^2)

  }
 ~~~~~~

 The atmospheric conditions can be retrieved at any time using \ref SolarPosition::getAtmosphericConditions().

 \subsubsection TurbidityDefinition Understanding the Turbidity Parameter

 The turbidity parameter used in the SolarPosition plugin is <b>Ångström's aerosol turbidity coefficient (β)</b>, the <b>aerosol optical depth at 1 \f$\mu\f$m</b>. It quantifies the amount of aerosols (dust, pollution, haze) in the atmosphere that scatter and absorb solar radiation.

 <b>Important:</b> This turbidity definition is NOT the same as "Linke turbidity" (T<sub>L</sub>), which is commonly used in some other solar radiation models and uses a different scale.

 The aerosol optical depth at wavelength λ follows the Ångström turbidity formula:
 \f[
 \tau_{aerosol}(\lambda) = \beta \lambda^{-\alpha}
 \f]
 where λ is in \f$\mu\f$m and α is the Ångström exponent, taken as 1.3 by the REST-2 and SSolar-GOA models and the sensor atmosphere model. The aerosol optical depth at 550 nm is therefore about 2.18β, and that at 500 nm about 2.46β; an aerosol optical depth measured at 500 nm (e.g., by a sun photometer) corresponds to β ≈ 0.41 times that value. The default turbidity is 0.02.

 The Prague sky model (\ref SolarPosition::updatePragueSkyModel()) is parameterized by ground-level visibility rather than aerosol optical depth. The aerosol optical depth at 550 nm is converted to visibility by log-log interpolation through the model's continental polluted, average and clean atmospheres, whose visibilities are 27.6, 59.4 and 131.8 km (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3450626.3459758">Wilkie et al. 2021</a>, Fig. 4) and whose OPAC aerosol optical depths at 550 nm are 0.327, 0.151 and 0.064 (Hess et al. 1998). The visibility is limited to the model's range of 20-131.8 km, so air cleaner than an aerosol optical depth at 550 nm of 0.064 (turbidity about 0.029, which includes the default of 0.02) is represented by the cleanest Prague atmosphere, and air hazier than about 0.45 (turbidity about 0.21) by the haziest. The visibility used is stored in Context global data as <code>prague_sky_visibility_km</code>.

 Higher turbidity values result in:
 - Reduced direct solar radiation
 - Increased fraction of diffuse radiation
 - Whitening of the sky (reduced blue color)
 - Enhanced circumsolar brightening (bright region around the sun)

 <table>
 <caption>Atmospheric condition parameters</caption>
 <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Description</th><th>Validation</th><th>Example Value</th></tr>
 <tr><td>pressure_Pa</td><td>Atmospheric pressure in Pascals (near the ground)</td><td>Must be > 0</td><td>101,325 Pa (1 atm)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>temperature_K</td><td>Air temperature in Kelvin (near the ground)</td><td>Must be > 0</td><td>300 K (27°C)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>humidity_rel</td><td>Air relative humidity (near the ground)</td><td>Must be 0-1</td><td>0.5 (50%)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>turbidity</td><td>Ångström's aerosol turbidity coefficient (β), the aerosol optical depth at 1 \f$\mu\f$m (see \ref TurbidityDefinition). <b>Note:</b> This is NOT Linke turbidity, which uses a different scale. Higher values indicate more aerosols in the atmosphere, which reduces direct solar flux and increases diffuse fraction.</td><td>Must be ≥ 0</td><td>0.02 (default)</td></tr>
 </table>

 \subsubsection OzoneColumn Total Column Ozone

 The REST-2 and SSolar-GOA models and the sensor atmosphere model (\ref SolarPosition::calculateSensorAtmosphereSpectra()) need the total column ozone, which absorbs in the ultraviolet and in the Chappuis band in the visible. It can be set, in Dobson units, with \ref SolarPosition::setOzoneColumn() (stored in Context global data as <code>atmosphere_ozone_DU</code>). Otherwise it is taken from a monthly zonal-mean climatology: the monthly mean total ozone of the NASA SBUV Merged Ozone Data Set (version 8.7; described for version 8.6 by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/2014JD021889">Frith et al. 2014</a>) in 36 latitude bands 5 degrees wide, averaged over 2005-2024. The value at the current date and latitude is interpolated linearly in latitude between the centers of the bands and in the day of the year between the middles of the months. It can be retrieved with \ref SolarPosition::getOzoneColumn().

 The climatology ranges from about 180 DU (the Antarctic ozone hole, 75-80 degrees south in October) to about 420 DU (the Arctic in spring). It has no data where the sun is too low for the satellite measurements, in high-latitude winter months, nor poleward of 80 degrees; for those dates and latitudes, requesting the ozone column raises an error, and it must be set with \ref SolarPosition::setOzoneColumn(). Next to a latitude band or month without data, the value of the band or month containing the latitude or date is used rather than interpolating toward the missing one.

 ~~~~~~
  sun.setOzoneColumn( 310.f ); //e.g., from a local measurement
 ~~~~~~

 \subsection SolarFlux Getting the Solar Flux

 The solar flux can be calculated using the REST-2 model of <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038092X07000990?casa_token=BAJYGez71awAAAAA:CfmA4oT9MLiHGvpD6oUkkDu4EJ1S9uRabZq4-wM07jtcmviZ12jvhD8VVcAkjLWoGNMtg8hDaqo">Gueymard (2008)</a> using the \ref SolarPosition::getSolarFlux() function. IT IS CRITICAL TO NOTE THAT THE CALCULATED FLUX IS FOR A SURFACE PERPENDICULAR TO THE SUN DIRECTION. To get the flux on a horizontal surface, multiply by the cosine of the solar zenith angle.

 Methods are available to get the incoming solar radiation flux perpendicular to the direction of the sun 1) for the entire solar spectrum (\ref SolarPosition::getSolarFlux()), 2) for the PAR band (\ref SolarPosition::getSolarFluxPAR()), and 3) for the NIR band (\ref SolarPosition::getSolarFluxNIR()).

 The very similar function \ref SolarPosition::getDiffuseFraction() calculates the fraction of the total flux that is diffuse. The fraction that is direct is simply one minus the diffuse fraction.

 Example code for using these solar flux functions is given below.

 ~~~~~~
  #include "SolarPosition.h"

  int main(){
      Context context; //declare the context

      //Set the current time and date
      context.setDate(1,5,2015); //May 1, 2015
      context.setTime(30,12); //12:30

      //Initialize the SolarPosition class
      SolarPosition sun( 7, 31.256, 119.947, &context );

      //Set atmospheric conditions globally
      sun.setAtmosphericConditions( 101000, 300, 0.6, 0.05 ); //pressure, temperature, humidity, turbidity

      //Get the sun position
      float zenith = sun.getSunZenith(); //zenithal angle (radians)

      //Calculate solar flux using stored atmospheric conditions
      float R = sun.getSolarFlux(); //flux perpendicular to sun (W/m^2)
      float R_horiz = R*cos(zenith);  //flux on horizontal surface

      float f_diff = sun.getDiffuseFraction(); //fraction of diffuse radiation

      float R_dir = R*(1.f-f_diff); //direct component of flux (W/m^2)

  }
 ~~~~~~

 \subsubsection SolarFluxTurb Calibrating the turbidity using weather station (radiometer) data

 The predicted solar flux may not perfectly match local predicted solar fluxes due to uncertainty in the local turbidity value. There is a built-in routine to calibrate the turbidity based on measured radiative fluxes.

 For the calibration, you must load radiation flux data into a timeseries within the Context. There must be at least one clear-sky day in the timeseries data, and the radiative fluxes must be for the entire solar spectrum in units of W/m<sup>2</sup>. You can then use the \ref SolarPosition::calibrateTurbidityFromTimeseries() method. This method takes one argument, which is a string corresponding to the timeseries variable name containing the radiation flux data.

 \subsubsection SolarFluxClouds Incorporating the effects of clouds

 The REST2 model for solar fluxes was developed for clear-sky conditions and cannot directly be used when clouds are present. If incident solar radiation data is available (e.g., from a weather station), this can be used to calibrate the model to account for the possible presence of clouds. A simple model is described below for doing so.

 Consider \f$R_{meas,h}\f$ to be the measured all-wave incoming solar radiation flux on a horizontal plane (clear or cloudy conditions), and \f$R_{clear}\f$ to be the predicted all-wave incoming solar radiation flux predicted by the REST2 model for clear-sky conditions perpendicular to the direction of the sun. This flux can be projected onto the horizontal plane according to

 \f[
    R_{clear,h} = R_{clear}\mathrm{cos}\,\theta_s.
 \f]

 The diffuse fraction is estimated from the hourly clearness index \f$k_T = R_{meas,h}/R_{0,h}\f$, where \f$R_{0,h}\f$ is the extraterrestrial irradiance on a horizontal surface, using the correlation of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0038-092X(82)90302-4">Erbs et al. (1982)</a>:

 \f[
    f_{diff} = \begin{cases}
      1.0 - 0.09\,k_T & k_T \leq 0.22 \\
      0.9511 - 0.1604\,k_T + 4.388\,k_T^2 - 16.638\,k_T^3 + 12.336\,k_T^4 & 0.22 < k_T \leq 0.80 \\
      0.165 & k_T > 0.80
    \end{cases}
 \f]

 The resulting flux that is output from the model is (flux perpendicular to the sun)

 \f[
   R_{model} = R_{clear}\frac{R_{meas,h}}{R_{clear,h}}.
 \f]

 Note that \f$R_{clear}\f$ is the all-wave (PAR+NIR) clear-sky flux, since the measured flux \f$R_{meas,h}\f$ is itself all-wave. The resulting attenuation factor \f$R_{meas,h}/R_{clear,h}\f$ is applied equally to the PAR and NIR bands, so \ref SolarPosition::getSolarFluxPAR() and \ref SolarPosition::getSolarFluxNIR() retain their clear-sky spectral ratio under calibration and continue to sum to \ref SolarPosition::getSolarFlux().

 \subsubsection SolarFluxCloudsSpectral Cloud calibration of the spectral model

 Cloud calibration also applies to the spectral (SSolar-GOA) model, i.e. to \ref SolarPosition::calculateDirectSolarSpectrum(), \ref SolarPosition::calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum() and \ref SolarPosition::calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum(). The approach follows <a href="https://doi.org/10.2172/6436871">Bird, Riordan and Myers (1987)</a>, who normalize a clear-sky spectral model against a measured broadband irradiance and then apply empirical spectral corrections. The procedure is:

 <ol>
 <li>The modeled clear-sky global spectrum is integrated over the model's 300-2600 nm window and compared with the measured broadband flux. Because a pyranometer sees essentially the whole shortwave spectrum while the model covers only this window, the measured value is scaled by the fraction of the extraterrestrial spectrum that falls inside it (0.9596) before the comparison.</li>
 <li>If the two agree to within 5%, no cloud modification is applied at all. Within this margin the discrepancy is dominated by measurement uncertainty and by error in the turbidity and precipitable-water inputs, so correcting for it would introduce noise rather than cloud effects. Bird et al. adopt the same criterion.</li>
 <li>Otherwise the direct and diffuse components are rescaled separately so that their sum matches the measurement and their ratio matches the diffuse fraction from the Erbs correlation above. Scaling both by a single factor would instead hold the diffuse fraction at its clear-sky value regardless of cloud thickness.</li>
 <li>The diffuse spectral shape is blended from clear-sky skylight toward the near-neutral direct-beam shape in proportion to how much of the diffuse light originates from cloud. Clear-sky skylight is Rayleigh-scattered and far bluer than light scattered by cloud droplets, which are large compared with the wavelength.</li>
 <li>The spectral modifiers of Bird et al. are applied to the diffuse component: an enhancement of \f$(\lambda+0.45)^{-1}\f$ for \f$\lambda \leq 0.55\,\mu\f$m (reaching \f$+1/3\f$ at \f$0.3\,\mu\f$m), and a constant 7% increase from 0.50 to 0.926 \f$\mu\f$m. The spectrum is then renormalized so it still integrates to the measured flux.</li>
 </ol>

 The net effect is that cloud both attenuates the spectrum and shifts it toward shorter wavelengths. The modeled photosynthetically active fraction of the irradiance in the model's 300-2600 nm window rises from about 0.45 under clear sky to about 0.51 under heavy overcast. The magnitude of this shift was calibrated against reported measurements of the PAR fraction of *broadband* shortwave irradiance, which rise from about 0.42 under clear sky to about 0.48 under overcast; note that the model's narrower wavelength window gives it a higher denominator-relative fraction than those broadband measurements, so the two sets of numbers are not directly comparable.

 \note This treatment is an empirical approximation, not a radiative-transfer calculation. It infers a spectral shape from a single broadband measurement, so it cannot represent the wavelength-selective absorption of cloud liquid water (notably the bands near 1450 and 1950 nm), and it assumes a fixed relationship between cloud thickness and spectral shift. The spectral modifiers of Bird et al. were derived from five days of measurements and validated only out to 1.1 \f$\mu\f$m; beyond that wavelength they are unconstrained by the original data. Where spectrally resolved accuracy under cloud is critical, use measured spectra rather than this model.

 In order to enable flux calibration for cloudy conditions, you must 1) Load timeseries data into the Context containing the measured all-wave solar radiation flux. This data must cover the entire period of the simulation. 2) Call \ref SolarPosition::enableCloudCalibration(), which requires a string corresponding to the timeseries data value reference in (1). Below is a code example:

 ~~~~~~
 #include "SolarPosition.h"

 int main(){
    Context context; //declare the context

    //Load radiation flux timeseries data from CSV file
    //assume that weatherdatafile.txt has columns given in the argument below, and has a mixture of clear-sky and cloudy days
    context.loadTabularTimeseriesData( "/path/to/weatherdatafile.txt", {"Date","Hour","R_tot_Wm2","Tair_C","humidity_rel","Patm_Pa"}, ",", "YYYYMMDD", 1 );

    //Initialize the SolarPosition class
    SolarPosition sun( 7, 31.256, 119.947, &context );

    //Enable cloud calibration
    sun.enableCloudCalibration( "R_tot_Wm2" ); //reference "R_tot_Wm2" data field in timeseries

    //Calibrate the clear-sky turbidity
    float turbidity = sun.calibrateTurbidityFromTimeseries( "R_tot_Wm2" ); //must have some clear-sky days in the weather data

    //Loop over time
    for( int t=0; t<context.getTimeseriesLength("R_tot_Wm2"); t++ ){
        context.setCurrentTimeseriesPoint( "R_tot_Wm2", t );

        float Tair_K = context.queryTimeseriesData( "Tair_C" ) + 273;
        float humidity_rel = context.queryTimeseriesData( "humidity_rel" );
        float Patm_Pa = context.queryTimeseriesData( "Patm_Pa" );

        sun.setAtmosphericConditions( Patm_Pa, Tair_K, humidity_rel, turbidity );

        //Calculate solar flux
        float R = sun.getSolarFlux(); //flux perpendicular to sun (W/m^2)

        float f_diff = sun.getDiffuseFraction(); //fraction of diffuse radiation

        float R_dir = R*(1.f-f_diff); //direct component of flux (W/m^2)
        float R_diff = R*f_diff; //diffuse component of flux (W/m^2)

    }
 }
 ~~~~~~

 An example of the above model applied to actual direct-diffuse partitioned radiation data using a shadowband radiometer is shown below. It should be emphasized that the above model is a relatively simple approximation that produces reasonable fluxes, but more accurate predictions are possible and require much more complicated models.

 \image html "CloudyFluxPartitioning.png"

 \subsection SpectralFlux Getting Spectral Solar Irradiance

 For applications requiring wavelength-resolved irradiance (e.g., photosynthesis models with wavelength-dependent quantum yield, remote sensing, hyperspectral image simulation), the \ref SolarPosition::calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum() "calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum()", \ref SolarPosition::calculateDirectSolarSpectrum() "calculateDirectSolarSpectrum()" and \ref SolarPosition::calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum() "calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum()" methods compute high-resolution spectral irradiance with the model described in \ref SpectralIrradianceTheory.

 This method uses the same atmospheric conditions set via \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions(), automatically deriving additional parameters (water vapor, ozone column) as needed. Results are stored in Context global data for use by other plugins (e.g., radiation plugin for ray tracing with spectral sources).

 The spectral model also depends on the albedo of the ground surrounding the scene. Light reflected by the ground is partly scattered back down by the atmosphere, which raises the global and diffuse irradiance, most strongly at blue and ultraviolet wavelengths where atmospheric scattering is strongest; without cloud calibration the direct beam is unaffected. The ground albedo defaults to 0.2 and can be set with \ref SolarPosition::setGroundAlbedo() (stored in Context global data as <code>atmosphere_ground_albedo</code>):

 ~~~~~~
  sun.setGroundAlbedo( 0.8f ); //e.g., fresh snow
 ~~~~~~

 Example code for calculating spectral irradiance:

 ~~~~~~
  #include "SolarPosition.h"

  int main(){
      Context context;

      //Set current time, date, and location
      context.setDate(16,7,2023); //July 16, 2023
      context.setTime(0,12); //Solar noon

      //Initialize SolarPosition with location
      SolarPosition sun( 0, 36.93f, 3.33f, &context ); //UTC, latitude, longitude

      //Set atmospheric conditions
      sun.setAtmosphericConditions( 87700.f, 298.f, 0.5f, 0.026f ); //pressure, temp, humidity, turbidity

      //Calculate global solar spectrum at 1 nm resolution (default)
      sun.calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum( "clear_sky" );

      //Or specify a coarser resolution (e.g., 10 nm)
      sun.calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum( "clear_sky_10nm", 10.0f );

      //Retrieve spectral data from Context using the same label
      std::vector<helios::vec2> global_spectrum;
      context.getGlobalData( "clear_sky", global_spectrum );

      //Use spectral data (wavelength in nm, irradiance in W/m²/nm)
      for( const auto& point : global_spectrum ){
          float wavelength_nm = point.x;
          float irradiance = point.y; //W/m²/nm on horizontal surface
          //... use wavelength-resolved irradiance
      }

      //Similarly for direct and diffuse components:
      sun.calculateDirectSolarSpectrum( "direct_beam" );
      sun.calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum( "sky_diffuse" );
  }
 ~~~~~~

 Three separate methods are available for the different spectral components:
 - \ref SolarPosition::calculateGlobalSolarSpectrum(): Total irradiance on horizontal surface (direct + diffuse)
 - \ref SolarPosition::calculateDirectSolarSpectrum(): Direct beam irradiance normal to sun direction
 - \ref SolarPosition::calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum(): Diffuse irradiance on horizontal surface

 Each method accepts an optional resolution parameter (default 1 nm) allowing wavelength downsampling. For example, resolution_nm=10.0 produces 231 wavelengths instead of the native 2301.

 \subsection SensorAtmosphere Simulating Satellite Imagery

 \ref SolarPosition::calculateSensorAtmosphereSpectra() computes the spectra needed to convert radiance leaving the scene into radiance at a sensor above the atmosphere (see \ref SensorAtmosphereTheory). Given the direction from the scene toward the sensor, it stores in Context global data the path radiance, the adjacency radiance, the upward direct and diffuse transmittances and the atmospheric spherical albedo, together with the direct, diffuse and global solar irradiance at the ground computed with the same atmosphere. To keep the illumination of the scene consistent with the atmosphere between it and the sensor, use these irradiance spectra, rather than those of \ref SolarPosition::calculateDirectSolarSpectrum() and \ref SolarPosition::calculateDiffuseSolarSpectrum(), for the scene's radiation sources.

 ~~~~~~
  SolarPosition sun( 0, 36.93f, 3.33f, &context );
  sun.setAtmosphericConditions( 101325.f, 298.f, 0.5f, 0.1f ); //pressure, temperature, humidity, turbidity
  sun.setGroundAlbedo( 0.15f ); //albedo of the ground surrounding the scene

  //Sensor at nadir
  sun.calculateSensorAtmosphereSpectra( "satellite", make_vec3(0,0,1) );

  std::vector<helios::vec2> path_radiance; //W/m^2/sr/nm
  context.getGlobalData( "satellite_path_radiance", path_radiance );
 ~~~~~~

 The turbidity set with \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions() (the Ångström coefficient β, see \ref TurbidityDefinition) is converted to an aerosol optical depth at 550 nm using an Ångström exponent of 1.3. Conditions outside the range of the look-up table (solar zenith above 70 degrees, view zenith above 60 degrees, aerosol optical depth at 550 nm above 1.2, surface pressure below 701.2 hPa or above 1050 hPa, or a water vapor path, the column water vapor times \f$1/\mu_s + 1/\mu_v\f$, above 35 g/cm²) and a sun below the horizon raise an error; pressures between 1013 and 1050 hPa are extrapolated linearly. The model assumes clear sky, so it cannot be used together with cloud calibration.

 \subsection SensorThermalAtmosphere Simulating Satellite Thermal Imagery

 \ref SolarPosition::calculateSensorThermalAtmosphere() computes the thermal infrared transmittance and upwelling and downwelling atmospheric radiance for a sensor viewing the scene from the given direction (see \ref SensorThermalAtmosphereTheory). They are stored in Context global data as spectra from 5502 to 15326 nm, and are applied to emission bands of a radiation camera by RadiationModel::enableCameraAtmosphere(). The atmospheric profile is built from the air temperature, humidity and pressure set with \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions() and the ozone column (\ref OzoneColumn); the column water vapor derived from the temperature and humidity is stored with the suffix "_thermal_water_vapor_cm". The model does not depend on the sun's position, so night-time imagery can be simulated.

 ~~~~~~
  sun.setAtmosphericConditions( 101325.f, 298.f, 0.5f, 0.1f );
  sun.calculateSensorThermalAtmosphere( "satellite", make_vec3(0,0,1) ); //sensor at nadir

  std::vector<helios::vec2> transmittance; //(wavelength in nm, transmittance)
  context.getGlobalData( "satellite_thermal_transmittance", transmittance );
 ~~~~~~

 The sky longwave radiance that the scene reflects in a thermal band should come from the same atmosphere. \ref SolarPosition::getThermalSkyFlux() gives the clear-sky longwave irradiance on a horizontal surface within a band, the band-specific counterpart of the broadband \ref SolarPosition::getAmbientLongwaveFlux(); it is the diffuse flux to give an emission band with the same wavelength bounds:

 ~~~~~~
  radiation.addRadiationBand( "TIR", 10600, 11190 ); //e.g., Landsat 8 TIRS band 10
  radiation.setDiffuseRadiationFlux( "TIR", sun.getThermalSkyFlux( 10600, 11190 ) );
 ~~~~~~

 \subsection LWFlux Getting the Sky Longwave Flux

 The downwelling longwave radiation flux from the sky can be calculated using the \ref SolarPosition::getAmbientLongwaveFlux() function. This function uses the air temperature and humidity set via \ref SolarPosition::setAtmosphericConditions(), in the same way as the \ref SolarPosition::getSolarFlux() function described above. The value returned by the function is the clear-sky downwelling longwave radiation flux on a horizontal surface in W/m<sup>2</sup>, integrated over all longwave wavelengths (see \ref LWTheory). It is the diffuse flux to give an emission band without wavelength bounds. For an emission band with wavelength bounds, use \ref SolarPosition::getThermalSkyFlux() instead, which gives the clear-sky irradiance within the band (see \ref SensorThermalAtmosphere):

 ~~~~~~
  radiation.addRadiationBand( "LW" ); //broadband
  radiation.setDiffuseRadiationFlux( "LW", sun.getAmbientLongwaveFlux() );

  radiation.addRadiationBand( "TIR", 10600, 11190 ); //e.g., Landsat 8 TIRS band 10
  radiation.setDiffuseRadiationFlux( "TIR", sun.getThermalSkyFlux( 10600, 11190 ) );
 ~~~~~~

*/
